const body = `<h1>Unit 1 — Setup & Movement</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Everything starts here: fork the starter, clone it, and take a character from zero to a moving, animated sprite. You arrive from two weeks of Scratch with the ideas already in your hands — sprites, loops, events — and Sep 14 is the day they land in a real engine. The three habits this unit builds — read before you run, predict before you change, tune one number at a time — carry through the entire year.</p>

<h2>Lessons</h2>
<table>
    <tr><th>ID</th><th>Title</th><th>What you build / learn</th></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/1.1.html">1.1</a></td><td>Launch: Fork, Clone &amp; Press Play</td><td>from GitHub fork to a running build on your machine — and a world where nothing has any code yet</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/1.2.html">1.2</a></td><td>The Unity Editor Tour</td><td>Hierarchy, Inspector, Scene, Play — the four windows you live in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/1.3.html">1.3</a></td><td>Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</td><td>fields, Start vs Update, Debug.Log as your eyes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/1.4.html">1.4</a></td><td>Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector</td><td>GetAxis → direction → vector → Translate, and your first working character</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/1.5.html">1.5</a></td><td>Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</td><td>frame-rate independence, live tuning, and choosing a movement method you can defend</td></tr>
        <tr><td><a href="../../lessons/1.6.html">1.6</a></td><td>Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack</td><td>animator parameters driven by code</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Concepts this unit owns</h2>
<p>This unit owns the <strong>movement pattern</strong> — input, direction, speed, deltaTime — that every controller you write this year reuses, and the <strong>predict–change–verify</strong> habit every lesson after it assumes. It also plants the problem the whole year grows from: the first movement method you reach for walks straight through walls, and you have to notice that and decide what to do about it. CED: CRD-2 (program purpose, design &amp; testing), AAP-2 (managing complexity with variables).</p>

<div class="unit-nav">
